body:
[Prop single_tree_size of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] Let $\tau$ be a Bienaymé tree with offspring distribution $\mu$. Let $\mathrm{h}$ be the largest common divisor of the support of $\mu$, and let $\mathrm{M}$ be the set of integers $m$ such that $\mathbf{P}(\#\tau=m)>0$. Then, there exists a constant $c> 0$ that only depends on $\sigma^2$ such that
   \begin{align*}
   \lim_{\substack{m\to\infty \\ m\in \mathrm{M}}} m^{3/2}\mathbf{P}(\#\tau=m)&=\mathrm{h}c,\\
   \quad \lim_{m\to\infty}m^{1/2}\mathbf{P}(\#\tau\geq m)&= 2c.
   \end{align*}

