{"artifact_digest":"sha256:2118cbb5c54951dcb3f50ae05b9bc0d659b7adc01058838e146e20a7596b4136","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/21/2118cbb5c54951dcb3f50ae05b9bc0d659b7adc01058838e146e20a7596b4136.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a8f566ba59a0396f08381c445f659482f1ee4da08f53ef1776c98cf71d97feac","bundle_entry_key":"2608-26642-theorem-10-prop2-6-eq1","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_112","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_112.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-26642-prop-10-prop2-6-eq1","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Define the operator $\mu:C(K)\to \R$ by $\mu f:=\mu[f]$. Let $g_1\in \ker (I-T^\mu)\cap \ker (\mu)$. Since $g_1=T^\mu g_1$, Lemma \ref{lem2.3} implies
\begin{align}
  0\le \int_K g_1^2 (x)\,\mu(dx)=\int_K g_1(x)\cdot T^\mu g_1(x)\,\mu(dx)=\mathscr{E}_h(g_1\cdot \mu)\le 0.
\end{align}
Thus, we have $g_1=0,\ \mu$-a.e. Since $g_1$ is continuous on $K$ and $\mathrm{supp}(\mu)=K$, we have $g_1\equiv 0$, that is, the operator $\mu|_{\ker (I-T^\mu)}$ is injective. Thus, by the rank-nullity theorem, we have
\begin{align}
\dim \Big(\ker (I-T^\mu)\Big)=\dim \Big(\mathrm{Im}(\mu|_{\ker (I-T^\mu)})\Big)+\dim\Big(\ker(\mu|_{\ker (I-T^\mu)})\Big)\le 1.
\end{align}
