[Theorem inv th1 of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] There exists a constant $\gamma\in (0,1)$ such that for each $t_0\in [0,T]$, one can find a constant $\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{C}(t_0)>0$ satisfying that

\begin{align} 
& |y (t_0)-\hat y(t_0)|_{L^2_{\mathcal{F}_{t_0}}(\Omega;L^2(G))}
 \leq
\mathcal{C} |y (\cdot)-\hat y(\cdot) |^{1-\gamma}_{L^2_{{\mathbb{F}}}(0,T;L^2(G))}
|y(T)-\hat y(T)|^{\gamma}_{L^2_{\mathcal{F}_T}(\Omega;H^1(G))},
\end{align}

where $y(\cdot)$ (resp. $\hat y(\cdot)$) solves the equation (sp-eq1 of the paper) with the initial datum $y_0$ (resp. $\hat y_0$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] For every $t \ge 0$ and $q \in \mcl Q_2^\sS$, we have
\begin{equation}  

\lim_{N \to +\infty} \bar F_N(t,q) = \sup_{p \in \mcl Q_\infty^\sS}\inf_{q' \in \mcl Q_\infty^\sS} \mcl J_{t,q}(q',p).
\end{equation}
Moreover, denoting this limit by $f(t,q)$, the function $f$ is the Lipschitz viscosity solution to
\begin{align}
\begin{cases}
    \partial_t f - \int_0^1 \xi(\partial_q f)=0 ,\qquad &\text{on }\R_+\times\mcl Q^\sS_2,
    \\
    f(0,\cdot) = \psi ,\qquad &\text{on }\mcl Q^\sS_2.
\end{cases}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[1 10 (\cite{HX26b}) of arXiv:2606.10058, 'Mean First Passage Times and Fundamental Tensors of Higher Order Markov Chains'] Let $\tilde {\cal P}$ be given as in (\ref{part}). Then, for any $k=1, 2, \ldots$, 
\be

\tilde{\cal P}^k=[\tilde{p}^{(k)}_{i_1i_2\ldots i_m}]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
{\cal Q}^k & {\cal O}\\
{\cal R}_k & {\cal I}
\end{array}\right],
\ee
where ${\cal R}_k$ satisfies 
$${\cal R}_1={\cal R}, ~{\cal R}_{k+1}={\cal R}_k \boxtimes {\cal Q} + {\cal R}.$$
Equivalently, we have the following partitioned tensor $\boxtimes$ multiplication: 
$$\left[\begin{array}{cc}
{\cal Q}^k & {\cal O}\\
{\cal R}_k & {\cal I}
\end{array}\right] \boxtimes \left[\begin{array}{cc}
{\cal Q} & {\cal O}\\
{\cal R} & {\cal I}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
{\cal Q}^{k+1} & {\cal O}\\
{\cal R}_{k+1} & {\cal I}
\end{array}\right].$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

