body:
[Theorem thm.main_basic of arXiv:2606.13442, 'Scaling limit of additive functionals for reversible non-gradient exclusion process: criti'] Under Hypothesis~(hyp of the paper) and under the stationary measure $\mathbb{P}_{\rho}$, for every centered local function $f$, the additive functional $(\Gamma^N_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ converges weakly in $C(\mathbb{R}_+, \mathbb{R})$ to a limit $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$, which is characterized  as follows:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item $d = 1$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a $\frac{3}{4}$-fractional Brownian motion with covariance matrix
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] = \frac{2\chi(\rho)}{3\sqrt{\pi \mathbf{D} (\rho)}}\overline{f}'(\rho)^2 \left(t^{\frac{3}{2}} + s^{\frac{3}{2}} - \vert t - s\vert^{\frac{3}{2}} \right).
			\end{align}
			\item $d = 2$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a Brownian motion with covariance
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] = \frac{\chi(\rho)\overline{f}'(\rho)^2}{2\pi\sqrt{\det[\mathbf{D}(\rho)]}} (t \wedge s).
			\end{align}
			\item $d \geqslant 3$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a Brownian motion with covariance
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] =  2 \left\Vert{f}\right\Vert^2_{-1} (t \wedge s).
			\end{align}
		\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 9 (\cite{QuanTrig}) of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] Fix $W_1,W_2, W_3 \ge \gamma^2/2$, and let $\beta_i= \gamma+\frac{2-W_i}{\gamma}$ if $W_i\neq \frac{\gamma^2}{2}$ and $\beta_i=Q^-$ if $W_i=\frac{\gamma^2}{2}$.
    Consider a distribution $\phi$ sampled from 
    \begin{equation*}
        \prod_{\beta_i\neq Q^-} (Q-\beta_i)^{-1} \LF_{\bH}^{(\beta_1,0), (\beta_2, 1),(\beta_3, \infty)}.
    \end{equation*}
    The infinite measure $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is defined to be the law of $(\bH, \phi, 0, 1, \infty)/\mathord\sim_\gamma$. A sample from $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is called a quantum triangle with weights $W_1$, $W_2$ and $W_3$.

[Proposition c:cltspindisag of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let \(\mathcal{P}\) be a simple, stationary point process on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with summable exponential mixing correlations as in Definition (def.A2 of the paper), and assume that the (reduced) Palm intensity function of the ground process \(\mathcal{P}\) is bounded at all orders, as specified in (equation bdedreduced of the paper). Let \(\Psi = \Psi(\cdot)\) be a stabilizing interaction graph on finite windows with respect to \(\mathcal{P}\) as in Definition~(d:stabilizing-graph of the paper), with finite averaged connective constant \(\bar\Delta<\infty\)  defined in Definition~(d:connective-constant-pp of the paper). If, for some $\epsilon>0$,  all sets \( B \in\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0) \)
defined in~(equation e.particular-sets of the paper), and almost every realization \(\mathcal{P} \), the maximum influence \( q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \) as in (equation defq of the paper) of the Gibbs specification~(equation eqn:gibbs of the paper) on $\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B)$ with finite spin space $\mathbb{K}$ satisfies the inequality 
\begin{equation} 
q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \le (\epsilon+\bar{\Delta})^{-1}
\end{equation}
 then the spin configuration \( \{V_x(\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n))\}_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n} \) satisfies the CLT as stated in Theorem~(spinmain of the paper), provided the variance condition there is also met. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

