[Proposition 21 (Random-bridge absorption probabilities) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $V_3,V_4,\dots$ be independent random variables with $V_m\sim\operatorname{Bern}(\frac{1}{m})$, $m\geq 3$.
Then, for $n\geq 2$ and $d\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \pbridge{n}{d}
    =\pr\left(\sum_{m=3}^{n}V_m\geq d\right).

\end{equation}
Consequently, for $n\geq 3$ and $d\geq 1$,
\begin{equation}
    \pbridge{n}{d}
    =\frac{n-1}{n}\pbridge{n-1}{d}
    +\frac{1}{n}\pbridge{n-1}{d-1}.

\end{equation}
Together with the boundary conditions  $\pbridge{n}{0}=1$  for all $n\geq 2$ and  $\pbridge{2}{d}=0$  for all $d\geq 1$, this
recurrence uniquely determines the array.

