[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2609.02064, 'Coalescence and fluctuations of O'Connell--Yor polymers\\ via the free-energy correlation '] For every compact $K\subset\R$,
  \begin{align}
    \sup_{u\in K}|\cZ_u^\eps-\cZ_u|\longrightarrow0.
  \end{align}
  The map $u\mapsto \cZ_u$ is continuous and strictly positive.
  Consequently, there are $\eps_K>0$ and $c_{K,\omega}>0$ such that
  \begin{align}
    \inf_{\substack{u\in K\\0\leq\eps\leq\eps_K}}\cZ_u^\eps
    \geq c_{K,\omega}.
  \end{align}

[Prop energy-est of arXiv:2411.06861, 'Quenched invariance principle for random walks in random environments admitting a cycle de'] Let $B$ be a finite subset of $\mathbb{Z}^d$. 
Consider a function $\eta: \mathbb{Z}^d\to \mathbb{R}$ with 
\begin{align*}
    \supp \eta \subset B, \quad 0\leq \eta \leq 1, \quad \eta \equiv 0  \text{ on } \partial B.
\end{align*}
Suppose $u: \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{Z}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ is either non-negative $L^\omega$-subharmonic $(-L^\omega u\leq 0)$,  or $L^\omega$-harmonic $(L^\omega u = 0)$ on $B$. Then we have for $p \in [1,\infty]$, there exists a constant $C_{\mathrm{En}} $ such that for all $t\in I $, 
\begin{align}


  \frac{\mathcal{E}_\Gamma^\omega(\eta u)}{\abs{B}}\leq C_{\mathrm{En}} \norm{\mu^{(2), \omega}}_{p, B} \, \norm{\nabla \eta}^2_{\ell_\infty(E)}  \norm{u^2}_{p_*,B}, 
\end{align}
with $p_*= p/(p-1)$ being the H\"{o}lder conjugate of $p$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop holderproper of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] We work under the assumptions of Theorem~(theo:convergence of the paper). Then, the following convergence in law in the space $\mathbb{D}([0,T], \mathbb{R})$ holds true
\begin{align*} 
\left(\langle \mu^{K}_{t}, \varpi \rangle\right)_{t \in [0,T]} \xrightarrow[K \longrightarrow + \infty]{}  \left(\langle \mu^{*}_{t}, \varpi \rangle\right)_{t \in [0,T]}. 
\end{align*}
As a consequence, using the results of Section~(subsec:tension of the paper) and Theorem~(theo:roel of the paper) again, we obtain that the process $\left((\mu^{K}_{t},R^{K}_{t})_{t \in [0,T]}\right)_{K \in \mathbb{N}^{*}}$ converges in law in $\mathbb{D}([0,T], (\mathcal{M}_{\varpi+\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+}),\mathrm{v}) \times [0,R_{\max}])$ towards $(\mu^{*}_{t},R^{*}_{t})_{t \in [0,T]}$.

[Prop Resolvent-Formula of arXiv:2411.19543, 'Feller property and convergence for semigroups of time-changed processes'] For every  $u\in\mathscr{B}_b(X)$ we have
\begin{equation}
	\check{R}_\alpha u(x) = \mathbb{E}_x\left[\int_0^\infty e^{-\alpha {\bf A}_t}u(\mathbb{X}_t)\,d{\bf A}_t \right],\quad \alpha>0.
\end{equation}
Assume in addition that $\mu$ is $G$-bounded and charges no semi-polar sets. Then 
\begin{equation}
	\check{R}_\alpha = (1 + \alpha G^\mu)^{-1} G^\mu,\quad \alpha\geq0.	

\end{equation}

