body:
[Definition of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] We say that $\eta\in \cB\setminus\{0\}$ is \emph{indecomposable} if for every decomposition
    \begin{align}
        \eta=\eta_1+\eta_2,\qquad \eta_1,\eta_2\in\cB,
    \end{align}
    there exists $a\in[0,1]$ such that $\eta_1=a\eta$ and $\eta_2=(1-a)\eta$.

[Prop 18 (\bf{the concentration inequality}) of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] Let $\{S_n\}$ be a random walk with increments $X_j$, $\ex X_j = 0$, $\ex X_j^2 = \sigma^2 \in (0, +\infty)$. Then there exists a real number $C$ such that for any $\Delta > 0$ there exists a natural $N$ such that for every $n > N$
\begin{equation}

\sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}}\pr\left(S_n \in [x, x + \Delta)\right) \le \frac{C \Delta}{\sqrt{n}}.
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
See, e.g. \cite{Petrov}, Section \RomanNumeralCaps{3}, Theorem 9.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

