body:
[Theorem thm:Z2L2bound2 of arXiv:2604.28068, 'Mean-square Stability and Bifurcations for Dissipative SDEs'] Let Assumption~(assum:FG_bounds of the paper) and (equation eq:diagonalize of the paper) hold, $Y$ be the solution of the centered SDE~(equation eq:main_YSDE of the paper) and $Z$ be the linearization error defined in (equation eq:Zdiff of the paper). Suppose there exists a non-decreasing
 function $K:[0,\infty)\to [0,\infty)$ such that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[\|R_F(Y(t),x^\ast)\|^2\right] \bigvee \expect{\|\mathrm{R_G}(Y(t),x^\ast)\|_{\mathbf{F}}^2} \leq K(t)  \qquad \text{for all } t\geq 0.
\end{equation}
 Then for any $\delta_1,\delta_2>0$ we have
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb{E}\left[\|Z(t)\|^2\right]\leq 
 \left[\left(1+\delta_1^{-1}+\delta_2^{-1}\right) K(t)t +\|Z(0)\|^2\right]
e^{\boldsymbol{\mu} t}\qquad \text{for all } t\geq 0,
\end{equation*}
where
\begin{equation}

\boldsymbol{\mu}:=2\lambda_{\max}\{\mathbf{D}F(x^\ast)\} + \delta_1 + (1+\delta_2)\|\mathbf{D}G(x^\ast)\|_{T_3}^2.  
\end{equation}
In particular, if $K$ is of subexponential growth and $\boldsymbol{\mu}<0$ 
then $\lim_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[\|Z(t)\|^2\right]=0.$

