[Definition def_weak_sol_eq_linear_prob of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] We say that $v$ is a weak solution of (equation PDE_problem_linear of the paper) if the following two conditions are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $v \in L^2([0,T], H^1([0,1]))$;
    \item For every $G \in C^{1,2}( {\Omega_T})$ and every $t \in [0,T]$, 
    \begin{equation} 
    F(v,G,t) = 0,
    \end{equation} where
    \begin{align*}
        F(v,G,t) &:= \int_0^1 v_t(x) G_t(x)dx - \int_0^t \int_0^1 v_s(x) \partial_s G_s(x) dx ds + \int_0^t \int_0^1 \nabla v_s(x) \nabla G_s(x) dx ds \\
        &- \int_0^t \int_0^1 v_s(x) f_s(x) \nabla G_s(x) dx ds - \sum_{y \in \{0,1\}} \int_0^t v_s(y)[h_s(y)-f_s(y)] G_s(y) ds.
    \end{align*} 
\end{enumerate}

