[Lem plus-start-final-step of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] The following holds for $0\le i\le k-1$ and for a $\beta$-independent constant $C$. If $\tau\sim \mathbf P$ for $\mathbf{P}\in \mathcal D(\Lambda_L^i)$, 
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbf{E}[\|\mu_{(i+1) t_L/k}^{+,i} - \pi_{\Lambda_L^i}^\tau \|] \le C(1+i) L^{-8}\,,
        \end{align*}
        where $\mu^{+,i}_t$ is the law of Ising dynamics in $\Lambda_L^i$ started from all plus. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

PRIVATE REDUCTION, author-recorded. Heydenreich-van der Hofstad Open Problem 13.4 asks that the second largest cluster at p_c(1 + eps) on {0,1}^d match, to leading order, the largest cluster at p_c(1 - eps), eps >> 2^{-d/3}. G(n,p) has this through the discrete duality principle: after the giant is removed, the rest is close in law to a subcritical G(n', p) with the dual mean (an asymptotic statement, not an exact symmetry); the hypercube has no such principle, so it must come from tails: the subcritical largest cluster is Theta(eps^{-2} log(eps^3 2^d)) once the tail P(|C| >= k) >= c k^{-1/2} exp(-c' eps^2 k) holds with the SHARP rate in eps^2 k (the upper bound is known, the sharp lower bound is the missing half), and the supercritical second-largest needs the same tail for the clusters after removal of the giant, which is the geometric-depletion question of the exploration process. So the duality principle is reduced, heuristically, to two tail statements, one subcritical with a sharp rate and one post-giant; turning tails into the size of the largest cluster also needs control of the number of clusters and of their dependence, so this is not an equivalence.
Bernoulli bond percolation on the hypercube {0,1}^d
Critical window width 2^{-d/3}

[Prop bdy-expect of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1]$. Let $D$ be a bounded, simply connected domain. Fix any $A\subseteq \partial D$ to be a (deterministic) closed subset of the boundary such that $\dim_M(A)<2-\alpha^2/2$. 
    For any continuous, bounded  function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$,
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb E[(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})]=0.
    \end{equation}
    As a consequence,
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})=0,
    \end{equation}
    where the limit is almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.

