[Definition 7 (Probability density of transition trajectories) of arXiv:2506.09462, 'Transition Path Theory For L\'{e}vy-Type Processes: SDE Representation and Statistics'] The \textbf{distribution of transition trajectories} from \(A\) to \(B\), denoted by \(\rho_{\mathcal{R}_{A \to B}} = (\rho_{\mathcal{R}_{A \to B}}(x))_{x \in \Theta}\), is defined such that for any \(x \in \Theta\),  
    \begin{equation}
        \rho_{\mathcal{R}_{A \to B}}(x) = \lim_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T \mathbf{1}_{x}(X_t) \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{R}_{A \to B}}(t) \d t,
    \end{equation}
    where \(\mathbf{1}_C(\cdot)\) denotes the characteristic function of the set \(C\).

[Corollary of arXiv:2604.23981, 'Accelerating sampling via asymptotic relaxation enhancing flows'] Suppose the invariant measure $\pi$ satisfies a log-Sobolev inequality, then under the conditions in Theorem (thm:entropydecay_are of the paper), one has
\begin{gather}
W_1(\rho(t),\pi)\le W_2(\rho(t),\pi)\le C_1\exp(-Bt/2).
\end{gather}

