body:
[Lemma lem:weak-overlap-sum of arXiv:2608.23135, 'Graph distance and effective resistance of the random walk trace in four and five dimensio'] For each fixed $R\ge2$, as $n\to \infty$, 
\begin{equation}


        \sum_{\substack{e,f\in\mathcal P_\vartheta:\,e\ne f,\,
        \ell(e),\ell(f)\le R,\\
        {\rm span}(e)\cap{\rm span}(f)\ne\emptyset}}
        \mathbb P(E_e\cap E_f)
        \longrightarrow0 .
\end{equation}
The corresponding product weight also tends to zero as $n\to \infty$: 
\begin{equation}

        \sum_{\substack{e,f\in\mathcal P_\vartheta:\,e\ne f,\,
        \ell(e),\ell(f)\le R,\\
        {\rm span}(e)\cap{\rm span}(f)\ne\emptyset}}
        q_eq_f
        \longrightarrow0 .
\end{equation}

[Thm 1 (Theorem 5.1 of Roynette--Vallois--Yor \cite{Roynette-Vallois-Yor-1}) of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Let $X$ be a standard Brownian motion. Suppose that $\mu$ is a finite positive Radon measure satisfying $\int_\R (1+|y|)\,\mu(dy)<\infty$. Then there exists a function $\phi^\mu$ such that
\begin{align}
\phi^\mu(x)=\lim_{t\to \infty}\sqrt{t}\P_x\Big[\Gamma_t^\mu\Big]\qquad \text{for}\ x\in \R
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\lim_{t\to \infty}\frac{\P_x[F_s\cdot \Gamma_t^\mu]}{\P_x[\Gamma_t^\mu]}=\P_x\left[F_s\cdot \frac{\phi^\mu(X_s)\Gamma_s^\mu}{\phi^\mu(x)}\right]\qquad \text{for}\ F_s\in b\F_s,
\end{align}
Moreover, the function $\phi^\mu$ is the unique solution to the Sturm--Liouville equation
\begin{align}

\frac{\Delta}{2}f=f\cdot \mu\qquad \text{in the weak sense,}
\end{align}
with boundary conditions
\begin{align}

  \lim_{x\to \infty}f'(x)=-\lim_{x\to -\infty}f'(x)=\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:main-finite-r-annular of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] Let \(0<s<1\), \(r>1\), and \(\delta>0\).  Let \(U\ge0\) satisfy \(\mathbb{E} U=1\) and (equation eq:finite-r-witnessed-gap of the paper).  Then there exist constants \(C,c,\eta>0\), depending only on \(s,r,\delta\) and the law of \(U\), such that, for every \(n\ge1\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left(
\sup_{2^n\le \lvert\xi\rvert\le 2^{n+1}}
\lvert\widehat{\nu_\circ}(\xi)\rvert>C2^{-sn/2}
\right)
\le
C\exp(-c2^{\eta n})+C2^{-cn}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\lvert\widehat{\nu_\circ}(\xi)\rvert=O(\lvert\xi\rvert^{-s/2})
\qquad (\lvert\xi\rvert\to\infty)
\end{equation}
almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

In the potential outcomes framework (Neyman-Rubin causal model), for each unit $i$ in a population and binary treatment $T_i \in \{0, 1\}$, there exist two potential outcomes: $Y_i(1)$, the outcome if the unit receives treatment ($T_i = 1$), and $Y_i(0)$, the outcome if the unit receives control ($T_i = 0$). The observed outcome is $Y_i = T_i Y_i(1) + (1 - T_i) Y_i(0)$.
Stable Unit Treatment Value Assumption (SUTVA)
Treatment is binary T \in {0, 1}

