{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e0198337b055248621eadd41ac56bf9888b3a251bb28a91840e43d31998c4b9d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e0/e0198337b055248621eadd41ac56bf9888b3a251bb28a91840e43d31998c4b9d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:8414e4b4c71e021ff0fb1adf0e2dea012be8d4870441b46ba12a1f5e3767a160","bundle_entry_key":"2604-01978-lemma-24-delta-method","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_006","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_006.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-01978-lemma-24-delta-method","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof is an application of the "delta method" to the dimensional parameters.

Define the random variables $X_d=(\<\bA x,\bA y\>,\|\bA x\|^{2},\|\bA y\|^{2})$, whose mean $m$ is $(\<x,y\>,1,1)$, and covariance $\Sigma$ is given by
\[
\Sigma
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bullet & \frac{2\<x,y\>}{d} & \frac{2\<x,y\>}{d} \\[0.7em]
\frac{2\<x,y\>}{d} & \frac{2}{d}&\frac{2\<x,y\>^{2}}{d} \\[0.7em]
\frac{2\<x,y\>}{d} &\frac{2\<x,y\>^{2}}{d}  & \frac{2}{d}
\end{bmatrix}+O(d^{-2}).
\]
Define $g(x,y,z)\coloneqq\frac{x}{\sqrt{yz}}$. A Taylor-Lagrange expansion shows
