body:
[Proposition p:V adapted of arXiv:2411.04773, 'Meeting of squared Bessel flow lines and application to the skew Brownian motion'] (i) Almost surely, for any $r\ge 0$: for any $x \in [V(-r),z)$
    \begin{equation}
        \widehat{\mathcal S}_{x,z}\circ \mathcal S_{-r,x}(0) >0.
    \end{equation}
    while for any $x\in [-r,V(-r))$, 
    \begin{equation}
         \widehat{\mathcal S}_{x,z} \circ\mathcal S_{-r,x}(0)=0.
    \end{equation}
    In particular,  with the convention that $\inf \emptyset=+\infty$, (see Figure (fig:meeting3pr of the paper))
    \begin{equation}
        V(-r)=\inf \{x\in[-r,z): \widehat {\mathcal S}_{x,z}\circ\mathcal S_{-r,x}(0)>0 \} \land z.
    \end{equation}

(ii)    For any $r\ge 0$, $V(-r)$ is measurable with respect to $\mathscr W^*_r$.

[Definition def:rwre of arXiv:2603.22652, 'A cohesive account on the ergodic behaviours and scaling limits of Random Walks in Cooling'] Let $\omega$ be sampled from $\mu$. The \emph{random walk in random environment}
(RWRE) is the Markov chain $Z=(Z_n)_{n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0}$ on ${\mathbb {Z}}$ with transition
kernel
\begin{equation}
P^{\omega}(Z_{n+1}=x+e\mid Z_n=x)=
\begin{cases}
\omega(x), & e=+1,\\
1-\omega(x), & e=-1,
\end{cases}
\qquad x\in{\mathbb {Z}},\ n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0.
\end{equation}
We write $P_x^{\omega}$ for the \emph{quenched} law of $Z$ started at $x$ and
\begin{equation}
P_x^{\mu}(\,\cdot\,):=\int_{(0,1)^{{\mathbb {Z}}}} P_x^{\omega}(\,\cdot\,)\,\mu(d\omega)
\end{equation}
for the corresponding \emph{annealed} law (with expectations $E_x^{\omega}$ and
$E_x^{\mu}$).

