{"artifact_digest":"sha256:abd597a657dced4a42f98fca2896b4acdaf72cd262a71468ef23bccfaa497b57","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ab/abd597a657dced4a42f98fca2896b4acdaf72cd262a71468ef23bccfaa497b57.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ae5ef1d23003ff9d22ba4ba87662ffb70d9ef08ba32c867b3a5617d8624e1f62","bundle_entry_key":"2606-16636-lemma-22-l-f-f-cn-gamma","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_052","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_052.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-16636-lemma-22-l-f-f-cn-gamma","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By a standard Gaussian interpolation argument, there exists a constant $C_1>0$ such that
\begin{align}
    \sup_{x,q}\Ll|\bar F^x_N(t,{q})-\bar F_N(t,{q})\Rr|\leq C_1N^{-1/16},\qquad \sup_{x,q}\Ll|\tilde F^x_N(t,{q})-\bar F_N(t,{q})\Rr|\leq C_1N^{-1/16}
\end{align}
for every $N\in\N$. For the details, we refer to the proof of~\cite[Lemma~6.4]{chen2025free}. That proof shows that the above differences vanish as $N\to\infty$. The stated rate is not written explicitly there, but it follows from the same estimates. By~\cite[Lemma~6.5]{chen2025free}, there exists a constant $C_2$ such that, for every $N\in\N$, $t\geq0$, and $q\in\mcl Q_1^\D$,
\begin{align*}
    \Ll|N\bar F_N(t,{q})-(N+1)\bar ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:ee62dc39d3618edeb9f0ba8bbdbbd1ae4f8cfcf0a6ab5b1112e77383ff82e98f","revision_digest":"sha256:831b9cca3aab9fb8e6034b344f9baa69bf3841a5b7e27a67bf5f2d7d2d33a66c","source_id":"arxiv:2606.16636","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

