{"artifact_digest":"sha256:58ae661085837bf39fec2a95bc0a1a0cbb585f340e8adb0f2c5ede980ba3377c","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/58/58ae661085837bf39fec2a95bc0a1a0cbb585f340e8adb0f2c5ede980ba3377c.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a3f7e3749af128fc6081a1b16ef181ba9be6f2f0478aaa2cce6416e7ff3b4961","bundle_entry_key":"2603-24616-lemma-25-lem-gibbsbox","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_001","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_001.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-24616-lemma-25-lem-gibbsbox","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Fix any $m \in \llbracket 1,k\rrbracket$ and $b \in \llbracket 1, T_1 \rrbracket$.  Suppose $\tilde{g}$ is an increasing path drawn in $\{ (r,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : 0 \leq r \leq b\}$ and $\vec{z} \in \mathfrak{W}_{2m}$, and they satisfy $\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, where $A$ denotes the event 
$$A =\{ \vec{z} = ({L}_{1}(b), \dots, {L}_{2m}(b)), L_{2m+1} \llbracket 0,b \rrbracket = \tilde{g} \}.$$
