[Lemma main_lemma_G of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] In the setting of (mix of the paper), suppose $H_a$, $a\in [\![{D}]\!]$, take the form
\begin{equation}
H_a = \sqrt{1-N^{-{\varepsilon}_g}}\cdot H_a^{(0)}+ {N^{-{\varepsilon}_g/2}} H_a^{(g)},
\end{equation}
where $H_a^{(0)}$ are independent Wigner matrices satisfying the assumptions for $H_a$ in (main_assm of the paper), and $H_a^{(g)}$ are i.i.d.~GUE satisfying (equation eq:meanvar of the paper) and (equation eq:meanvar2 of the paper). Then, for small enough constant ${\varepsilon}_g>0$ (depending on $\delta_A$ and ${\varepsilon}_A$) and $z=E+\mathrm{i}\eta $ with $E=\gamma_k$ for some $k\le DN/2$, there exists an absolute constant $C>8\vee C_0$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\|\mathbb{E} L_{(1,2)}- K_{(1,2)}\|_{2}\prec N^{-1-{\varepsilon}_g}\eta^{-2},\quad \forall \  N^{-2/3+C{\varepsilon}_g }k^{-1/3} \le \eta \le N^{-C{\varepsilon}_g }\ , \ z_1,z_2\in \{z, \overline z\}.
\end{equation}

[Lm 5 of arXiv:2609.02576, 'Sharp estimates for the Jacobi and trigonometric Dunkl heat kernels'] Let $\psi\in C^2((0,\infty)^3)$. The following formula is valid
	\begin{multline*}
		\frac{D H^\psi_t(x,y)}{H^\psi_t(x,y)}\\
		= \big( G(x)-1\big) \Big( \frac{x-y}{xt} -\frac{2 \eth_x \psi}{x}\Big) +\eth_t\psi +\frac{x-y}{xt} +\Big(\frac{x-y}{t} -\frac{2}{x}\Big)\eth_x \psi -\eth^2_x\psi.
	\end{multline*}

