[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2608.25094, 'Noising-Denoising by Large Temperature Schr\"{o}dinger Bridges'] In the setting of Section \ref{sec:ou-diff}, let $\mu = N(0,1)$ and $\nu = N(0,\eta^2)$. Then $P^{T}$ and $Q^{T}$ can be explicitly computed, and setting $\pi^{T} = (x_0,x_{T})_{\#}P^{T}$ and $q^{T} = (x_0,x_{T})_{\#}Q^{T}$ we compute
    \begin{align*}
        \lim\limits_{T \to +\infty }\exp(2T)\left(H(\pi^{T}|q^{T})+H(q^{T}|\pi^{T})\right) < +\infty.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, the rate for the endpoint distributions suggested by Theorem \ref{thm:euclidean-case} is not in general tight.

